1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,386
The whole production stack runs on one laptop, with one command.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:16,374
Make up starts fifteen containers: the edge, the gateway, the site, the meter's database, the model server, and the tools that watch them, from metrics and logs to Grafana and Langfuse.

3
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:27,472
The model server is llama dot cpp on the laptop's GPU, running Qwen3, thirty billion parameters, in twenty-one of the card's twenty-four gigabytes.

4
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:29,812
Every question tries it first.

5
00:00:29,862 --> 00:00:33,367
A local answer costs nothing, and nothing leaves the machine.

6
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:37,607
Only when it's down does the capped paid fallback answer.

7
00:00:37,657 --> 00:00:42,853
On the cloud server, which has no GPU, the Anthropic API answers first.

8
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:51,276
This is production, not a demo: every image pinned by its digest, and one build at one commit, the same image everywhere.

9
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:54,450
Offline-first isn't new for Hareet.

10
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:01,811
At Medic, the community health workers using the Community Health Toolkit had older Android phones, and mostly no network.

11
00:01:02,310 --> 00:01:09,157
So Hareet designed Voice for CHT: the health worker speaks the visit, and the form fills itself, offline.

12
00:01:09,207 --> 00:01:17,145
Hareet helped the team fine-tune its models to two hundred seventy-three megabytes, to run beside the app on a four-gigabyte phone.

13
00:01:17,195 --> 00:01:20,815
No audio or transcript leaves the phone as telemetry.

14
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:30,157
At denverlocal dot A I, the same idea serves small Denver teams: private AI, with sensitive files kept on hardware they own.

15
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:34,779
Local models first. Built in the open, at hareet dot A I.
